    "concept": "recursion",
    "description": "Self-targeting invocation — a function calling itself directly or indirectly",

    "universal_shape": {
        "function": "the function that calls itself",
        "base_case": "condition that terminates recursion",
        "recursive_call": "the self-invocation with modified arguments",
        "accumulator": "optional state carried through calls"
    },

    "semantic_notes": [
        "Rust: fn foo() { ... foo() ... } — direct recursion. Tail call not guaranteed.",
        "Go: func foo() { ... foo() ... } — direct recursion. No tail call optimization.",
        "C: void foo() { ... foo(); ... } — direct recursion. Stack-limited.",
        "Composites function_call + self-reference, but like self_access composites method_call + self-reference.",
        "Distinct behavioral property worth tracking — determines algorithm complexity class.",
        "Recursion without a base case is infinite — the halt condition matters."
    ],

